CertHub.register({"id":"data-plus","vendor":"CompTIA","name":"CompTIA Data+","short":"Data+","exam":"DA0-002","blurb":"Entry-level data analysis certification for people who collect, clean, analyze, visualize and govern data: data concepts, acquisition and preparation, statistics, reporting and governance.","status":"check","statusNote":"Domains and weights come from the published DA0-002 exam objectives as best known (Data concepts and environments 20%; Data acquisition and preparation 22%; Data analysis 24%; Visualization and reporting 20%; Data governance 14%). The CompTIA site could not be opened from this environment, so confirm the domains, weights, question count and passing score on the official Data+ page before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-25","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"Up to 90 (multiple choice and performance-based)","minutes":90,"pass":"Check the official page (DA0-001 required 675 on a 100–900 scale)","extra":"No formal prerequisites. CompTIA suggests some experience working with data, such as 1–2 years in a report, business or data analyst role. The certification is valid for 3 years and can be renewed through continuing education."},"examSim":{"questions":90,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CompTIA Data+ certification page (exam details and objectives)","url":"https://www.comptia.org/certifications/data"}],"planWeeks":8,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Data concepts and environments","w":20,"topics":["Structured, semi-structured and unstructured data, with examples of each","Relational databases: tables, primary and foreign keys, normalization and relationships","Non-relational databases: document, key-value, column-family and graph stores","Data types: strings, integers, decimals, dates and times, Booleans and type conversion","File formats: CSV, TSV, JSON, XML, Parquet, spreadsheets and flat files","Data warehouses, data marts, data lakes and lakehouses; OLTP vs OLAP","Dimensional modeling: fact and dimension tables, star and snowflake schemas, slowly changing dimensions","Data environments: on-premises vs cloud, and tools such as spreadsheets, SQL clients, notebooks and BI platforms","Languages for analysis: SQL, Python and R, and when to use each","AI and automation concepts for analysts: machine learning, generative AI, large language models, NLP and RPA"],"notes":["Domain 1.0 of the DA0-002 objectives"],"labs":["Install the free DB Browser for SQLite, create Customers and Orders tables with a primary key and a foreign key, insert a few rows and confirm the foreign key rejects an order for a customer that does not exist (run `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;` first).","In a free Google Colab notebook (or a local Jupyter install), load the same small dataset from CSV and from JSON with pandas, then compare `df.dtypes` and fix any column that was read with the wrong type, such as a ZIP code read as a number.","Open the free Google BigQuery sandbox (no credit card needed; stay inside the sandbox limits and delete any datasets you create when you finish), query one of the public datasets and sketch its fact and dimension tables as a star schema."]},{"id":2,"name":"Data acquisition and preparation","w":22,"topics":["Data acquisition methods: database extracts, APIs, web scraping, file exports, surveys and sampling","ETL vs ELT and data pipelines: batch vs streaming, full vs incremental loads","Writing SQL queries: SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY and HAVING","Combining data: inner, left, right and full joins, unions and appending","Data quality problems: duplicates, missing values, invalid values, outliers, inconsistent formats and redundancy","Handling missing data: deletion, imputation and flagging","Data transformation: parsing, splitting and concatenating fields, type conversion, recoding and derived variables","Scaling and grouping: normalization, standardization, binning and aggregation","Reshaping data: pivoting and unpivoting, wide vs long format, filtering and sorting","Query optimization: indexing, filtering early, avoiding SELECT *, subsets and temporary tables"],"notes":["Domain 2.0 of the DA0-002 objectives"],"labs":["In DB Browser for SQLite, load a free public CSV (for example a city open-data file), then write queries that use WHERE, GROUP BY with HAVING, and an INNER vs LEFT JOIN against a small lookup table you create; compare the row counts and explain the difference.","In Google Colab with pandas, take a messy sample file and fix it step by step: `drop_duplicates()`, trim and standardize text with `str.strip().str.title()`, convert dates with `pd.to_datetime`, impute missing numbers with the median and add a flag column for imputed rows.","In the BigQuery sandbox (free, no billing account; delete your datasets afterward), run the same aggregate query with `SELECT *` and with only the needed columns and a date filter, and compare the bytes processed that the query editor reports."]},{"id":3,"name":"Data analysis","w":24,"topics":["Measures of central tendency: mean, median and mode, and how skew affects them","Measures of dispersion: range, variance, standard deviation, interquartile range and percentiles","Distributions: normal distribution, skewness, the empirical rule and z-scores","Descriptive statistics in practice: counts, frequencies, percentages, percent change and ratios","Inferential statistics: samples vs populations, confidence intervals, hypothesis testing and p-values","Type I and Type II errors, statistical significance and sample size","Correlation vs causation, and simple linear regression","Types of analysis: exploratory, descriptive, diagnostic, predictive, prescriptive and trend analysis","Performance analysis: KPIs, metrics, targets and variance to plan","Choosing analysis tools and functions: spreadsheet formulas, SQL aggregates and window functions, Python and R libraries","Checking and troubleshooting results: sanity checks, reconciliation, and common calculation errors"],"notes":["Domain 3.0 of the DA0-002 objectives"],"labs":["In LibreOffice Calc or Google Sheets (both free), compute AVERAGE, MEDIAN, MODE, STDEV.S and QUARTILE for a salary column, add one extreme value and record which measures move the most.","In Google Colab, use pandas `describe()` and `corr()` on a public dataset, draw a scatter plot of two correlated columns, and fit a simple linear regression with `numpy.polyfit` to predict one value from the other.","In the BigQuery sandbox or SQLite, write a query with `SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day)` for a running total and a month-over-month percent change, then reconcile the grand total against a plain `SUM` to check your work."]},{"id":4,"name":"Visualization and reporting","w":20,"topics":["Choosing a chart: bar, line, pie, scatter, histogram, box plot, heat map, map and table","Dashboard design: layout, audience, KPIs, filters, drill-down and interactivity","Design principles: color, labels, titles, scales and axes, accessibility and avoiding misleading charts","Report types: static vs dynamic, ad hoc vs recurring, self-service and executive summaries","Communicating findings: knowing the audience, storytelling with data and stating limitations","Report elements: cover information, methodology, data sources, refresh dates, disclaimers and appendices","Delivery and refresh: scheduled refresh, real-time vs snapshot data, subscriptions and distribution","Report versioning, style guides and corporate branding","Troubleshooting reports and dashboards: stale data, broken filters, wrong totals and slow performance"],"notes":["Domain 4.0 of the DA0-002 objectives"],"labs":["Install the free Power BI Desktop (Windows) or use free Tableau Public, load a sample sales dataset and build a one-page dashboard with three KPI cards, a line chart, a bar chart and a region filter. Remember that anything saved to Tableau Public is visible to everyone, so use only public sample data.","Take one chart from your dashboard and make a deliberately misleading version (truncated axis, 3D pie, rainbow colors), then fix it and write two sentences on what changed.","Write a one-page executive summary of your dashboard with a headline finding, a recommendation, the data source, an as-of date and one stated limitation; ask a friend who has not seen the data whether the main point is clear in 30 seconds."]},{"id":5,"name":"Data governance","w":14,"topics":["Data governance roles: data owner, data steward, data custodian and data consumer","Metadata, data dictionaries, data catalogs and data lineage","Data quality dimensions: accuracy, completeness, consistency, validity, timeliness and uniqueness","Data quality control: validation rules, profiling, quality metrics and monitoring","Master data management and a single source of truth","Sensitive data: PII, PHI and payment card data; data classification levels","Privacy and compliance: GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS, data sovereignty and retention policies","Protecting data: access control and least privilege, masking, anonymization, pseudonymization and encryption","Data life cycle: collection, storage, use, sharing, archiving and secure disposal"],"notes":["Domain 5.0 of the DA0-002 objectives"],"labs":["Write a data dictionary in a free spreadsheet for a dataset you used earlier: column name, business definition, data type, allowed values, owner, sensitivity level and source system.","In Google Colab, profile a dataset with pandas (null counts, distinct counts, min and max, value counts) and write three validation rules as code that print the rows that fail each rule.","In SQLite or pandas, create a masked copy of a fake customer table: replace names with random IDs (pseudonymization), keep only the last four digits of a made-up card number and generalize birth dates to birth year. Use only invented data, never real personal data."]}],"study":{"1":[["Give one example each of structured, semi-structured and unstructured data.","Structured: rows in a SQL table with fixed columns. Semi-structured: JSON or XML, which has tags or keys but a flexible shape. Unstructured: emails, PDFs, images, audio and video with no data model."],["What is the difference between a primary key and a foreign key?","A primary key uniquely identifies each row in its own table and cannot be null. A foreign key is a column in another table that refers to that primary key, which links the tables and enforces referential integrity."],["Compare a data warehouse, a data mart and a data lake.","A warehouse holds cleaned, integrated, structured data from many sources, modeled for reporting (schema on write). A data mart is a smaller subject-area slice of a warehouse, such as sales. A data lake stores raw data of any type in its native format and applies structure when read (schema on read)."],["Explain OLTP vs OLAP.","OLTP systems run the business: many small, fast inserts and updates, usually normalized. OLAP systems analyze the business: fewer, larger read-heavy queries over history, usually denormalized star schemas."],["In a star schema, what goes in the fact table and what goes in the dimension tables?","The fact table holds measurable events at a chosen grain (quantity, amount) plus foreign keys. Dimension tables hold descriptive context used to filter and group (date, product, customer, store)."],["When would you reach for SQL, Python or R?","SQL to query, join and aggregate data where it lives in a database. Python for general data wrangling, automation and machine learning with libraries such as pandas. R for statistical analysis and statistical graphics. Many analysts use SQL plus one of the other two."]],"2":[["What is the difference between ETL and ELT?","ETL transforms data in a separate step before loading it into the target. ELT loads raw data first and transforms it inside the target, usually a cloud warehouse with a powerful SQL engine."],["Why can't you use an aggregate in a WHERE clause, and what do you use instead?","WHERE filters individual rows before grouping, so aggregates do not exist yet. HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY, for example HAVING SUM(sales) > 1000."],["What does a LEFT JOIN return that an INNER JOIN does not?","All rows from the left table, including those with no match on the right; the right-side columns are NULL for them. An INNER JOIN returns only rows that match in both tables."],["Name three ways to handle missing values and a risk of each.","Delete rows (listwise deletion): loses data and can bias results if missingness is not random. Impute with mean or median: understates variance; the mean is pulled by outliers. Flag and keep: needs later steps that can handle the flag or blanks."],["Normalization vs standardization: what does each do?","Min-max normalization rescales values to a fixed range, usually 0 to 1. Standardization converts values to z-scores with mean 0 and standard deviation 1. Both put variables on comparable scales."],["What is the difference between pivoting and unpivoting?","Pivoting turns row values into columns (long to wide), for example one column per month. Unpivoting turns columns into rows (wide to long), producing attribute and value columns that most BI tools prefer."]],"3":[["When is the median a better summary than the mean?","When the data is skewed or has outliers, such as incomes or house prices. The mean is pulled toward extreme values; the median is the middle value and is not."],["What does the empirical rule say?","For a roughly normal distribution, about 68% of values fall within 1 standard deviation of the mean, about 95% within 2 and about 99.7% within 3."],["How do you calculate percent change, and what is a common mistake?","(new − old) ÷ old × 100. A common mistake is dividing by the new value instead of the old one, or confusing percent change with percentage-point change."],["Explain a p-value in plain language.","Assuming the null hypothesis is true, the p-value is the probability of seeing a result at least as extreme as the one observed. If it is below the chosen significance level (often 0.05), you reject the null hypothesis."],["What is the difference between a Type I and a Type II error?","Type I: rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive). Type II: failing to reject a false null hypothesis (false negative). Lowering the significance level reduces Type I errors but, all else equal, raises Type II errors."],["Describe the four main analysis types with a question each answers.","Descriptive: what happened? Diagnostic: why did it happen? Predictive: what is likely to happen? Prescriptive: what should we do about it?"]],"4":[["Which chart would you use for a trend over time, a comparison across categories, a relationship between two numbers and a distribution?","Trend: line chart. Categories: bar or column chart. Relationship: scatter plot. Distribution: histogram or box plot."],["Name three ways a chart can mislead.","A truncated y-axis that exaggerates differences, 3D or area effects that distort size, inconsistent scales between charts, cherry-picked time ranges, or too many pie slices."],["What should an executive summary contain?","The main finding, why it matters, a recommendation or next step, and a short note on the data and its limitations, all on one page before the detail."],["Static vs dynamic reports: when would you use each?","Static reports (PDF, slide) capture a fixed snapshot for a period, good for formal records and distribution. Dynamic reports and dashboards connect to data, refresh and let users filter and drill down, good for ongoing monitoring."],["A dashboard shows yesterday's numbers when it should show today's. What would you check?","Whether the scheduled refresh ran and succeeded, the credentials and gateway or connection used by the refresh, whether the upstream pipeline loaded new data, and any date filter or cached extract."]],"5":[["Contrast the data owner, data steward and data custodian.","The owner is the accountable business leader who decides classification and access. The steward manages definitions, quality and proper use day to day. The custodian (often IT) implements the technical controls: storage, backups, permissions and encryption."],["What is data lineage and why do auditors care?","Lineage records where data came from and every transformation it went through to reach a report. It lets you trace and prove how a number was produced and assess the impact of changes."],["List the common data quality dimensions.","Accuracy (matches reality), completeness (no missing required values), consistency (agrees across systems), validity (fits allowed formats and ranges), timeliness (current enough) and uniqueness (no duplicates)."],["Anonymization vs pseudonymization?","Anonymization irreversibly removes the ability to identify a person. Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with tokens or IDs, but a separately stored key can re-identify people, so under GDPR pseudonymized data is still personal data."],["Which regulation fits each: EU personal data, US health records, payment cards?","GDPR for personal data of people in the EU; HIPAA for protected health information held by US covered entities and their business associates; PCI DSS for organizations that store, process or transmit payment card data."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasQuestionsEs":true});
